package Vixen.Shaders.PostFx

import Vixen.Shaders.Core
import Vixen.Shaders.Geometry

// For `ShadowCascade` and `Lighting`, which is where the reverse-Z compare and the two biases live.
import Vixen.Shaders.Shading

// ⚠ Imported for `FrameShadow`, and it is the right edge rather than a convenient one. Cascade
// *selection* is the part of shadowing that is easy to restate and fatal to restate differently —
// containment rather than depth, the two-column fold, the edge blend, the fade at the shadow
// distance — and `FrameLit.rvn` already carries the transliteration of `ClusteredShading`'s version
// with `TerrainShaderParityTests` holding it against drift. A froxel that selected its cascade by a
// second copy of that rule would shadow the air on one side of the terrain boundary and the ground
// on the other. The struct takes every resource as a parameter precisely so a pass in another set
// can call it, which is the case this is.
import Vixen.Shaders.Terrain

// ⚠ Imported for `WaterVolume.Phase` and for nothing else. Henyey–Greenstein is a function of an
// angle and an anisotropy, and it does not know whether the medium is a lake or a valley — so the
// alternative to this edge is a second copy of an eight-line function that a fixture already covers,
// and two copies of one definition is two places for it to drift. `PostFx` already imports across
// packages for `DistanceFields` and `IrradianceFields`.
import Vixen.Shaders.Water

// Froxel volumetric fog: the three compute stages that fill a volume the composite then reads.
//
// `docs/plan/06-rendering-pipeline.md` § P2. Two shaders and three dispatches, and the seam between
// them is not arbitrary: `VolumetricFogInject` reads nothing and the other two sample a volume. A set
// is written whole or not at all, so a pass that shares a set with a sampler it never uses still has
// to bind something into it — see `VolumetricFogInject`'s own note for what that cost on a device.
// The two that *do* sample share a set and a `Pass` permutation, on `AutoExposure`'s terms.
//
// **What is reused, and what the plan document got wrong.** It says this "shares the clustering
// grid". Half of that is right. The Z distribution is the cluster grid's — `near · (far/near)^(k/n)`,
// and `DepthOf`'s negation with it — and a froxel finds its light list through `ClusterGrid.Of`. But
// the grid itself is 16×9×24 with a light capacity of 32, and it is a *structured buffer*: far too
// coarse for fog, and the wrong kind of resource for a stage that wants to filter its neighbours.
// This owns its own volumes, at its own resolution, over its own near and far.
//
// **The far plane is this shader's own and not the camera's**, which is the single most important
// number here. A froxel grid over a camera's whole range spends most of its slices on distance no fog
// is visible at, and the near slices — where every visible shaft actually is — end up thinner than a
// metre apart. Sixty-four to a hundred metres is the useful range; beyond it the composite falls back
// to the analytic falloff `Fog.rvn` has always had.
//
// The scattering arithmetic is `WaterShading.rvn`'s, deliberately and without re-derivation: the
// Henyey–Greenstein phase and Beer–Lambert transmittance are the same functions of the same
// quantities whether the medium is a lake or a valley, and that file's versions have a fixture behind
// them. What is different is the integration — water composites one homogeneous slab in closed form,
// and this marches an inhomogeneous one — which is why `WaterVolume.InScatter` is not called here.

/// The froxel grid's shape and the arithmetic for finding a froxel in it.
///
/// Permutations rather than constants, unlike `ClusterGrid`: nothing here sizes a struct, so the
/// dimensions are free to be a quality decision. A tier picks them, the host declares volumes to
/// match, and the variant is recompiled — which is the point, because the loop bound in `Integrate`
/// wants to be a constant.
struct FroxelGrid {
    /// The view-space depth at which slice `k` begins. `ClusterGrid.SliceDepth`, verbatim.
    ///
    /// Exponential and not uniform: a uniform split spends most of its slices on distance nothing
    /// occupies, and this gives every slice the same *ratio* of depths, which is how perspective
    /// actually distributes what a camera sees. Invertible in closed form, so `SliceOf` is a
    /// logarithm rather than a search.
    static func SliceDepth(slice: int, slices: int, near: float, far: float): float {
        return near * pow(far / near, float(slice) / float(slices))
    }

    /// Which slice a view-space depth falls in — `SliceDepth`'s inverse, unclamped.
    ///
    /// Fractional, because the composite wants to filter between two slices rather than land in one.
    /// The caller clamps: a pixel beyond the fog's far plane has no slice, and that is the case the
    /// analytic fallback exists for rather than something to round into the last one.
    static func SliceOf(viewDepth: float, slices: int, near: float, far: float): float {
        val ratio = log(max(viewDepth, near) / near) / max(log(far / near), Const.Epsilon)
        return ratio * float(slices)
    }

    /// How far in front of the camera a view-space position is.
    ///
    /// **Negated, because the engine's view space is right-handed** — `Matrix4x4.LookAt` puts the
    /// camera looking down −Z, so a visible point's `z` is negative while every depth here is a
    /// positive distance. `ClusterGrid.DepthOf` is the same line, and its own comment records that
    /// this sign has already cost one debugging round.
    static func DepthOf(positionVS: float3): float => -positionVS.z

    /// The view-space ray through a point on the grid. `ClusterGrid.Ray`, verbatim.
    ///
    /// ⚠ `z` is −1, so this is a *direction whose view depth is one*: multiplying it by a depth gives
    /// the view-space position at that depth directly, and its length is how much further a metre
    /// along the ray is than a metre of depth. `Integrate` needs exactly that ratio to turn a slice's
    /// depth range into a path length.
    static func Ray(uv: float2, tanHalfFov: float2): float3 {
        val ndc = uv * 2f - 1f
        return float3(ndc.x * tanHalfFov.x, ndc.y * tanHalfFov.y, -1f)
    }

    /// The grid UV a froxel's centre sits at, in the grid's own coordinates.
    ///
    /// ⚠ No y flip, and `ClusterGrid.UvOf` says the same about its own pair: this is not a texture
    /// coordinate for anything but the volumes, and what it must be is the exact inverse of the ray
    /// `Ray` builds. The composite in `Fog.rvn` converts once, where a screen UV meets the volume.
    static func Uv(x: int, y: int, gridX: int, gridY: int): float2 {
        return float2((float(x) + 0.5f) / float(gridX), (float(y) + 0.5f) / float(gridY))
    }

    /// The view depth halfway through slice `z`, in the ratio the slices are cut by.
    ///
    /// ⚠ The *geometric* midpoint and not the arithmetic one, because the slices are geometric: the
    /// centre of `[near·rᵏ, near·rᵏ⁺¹]` under a ratio split is `near·rᵏ·√r`, which is the half-power
    /// factored out here. Sampling the medium at one midpoint and lighting it at another is a volume
    /// whose density and whose illumination describe different points — invisible in a uniform fog
    /// and a half-slice offset in every shaft.
    static func CentreDepth(z: int, slices: int, near: float, far: float): float {
        return SliceDepth(z, slices, near, far) * pow(far / near, 0.5f / float(slices))
    }

    /// The view-space position of a froxel's centre — the one point the whole grid agrees on.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Injection and lighting must reach this through the same function.** They are separate
    /// dispatches over the same grid, and a froxel whose coefficients were written for one position
    /// and whose in-scatter was computed at another is the failure `Fog.rvn` records about its own
    /// three numbers: smooth, plausible, and wrong everywhere.
    static func Centre(
        x: int,
        y: int,
        z: int,
        gridX: int,
        gridY: int,
        gridZ: int,
        near: float,
        far: float,
        tanHalfFov: float2
    ): float3 {
        return Ray(Uv(x, y, gridX, gridY), tanHalfFov) * CentreDepth(z, gridZ, near, far)
    }
}

/// The medium's coefficients, written into a volume. The one pass that reads nothing.
///
/// ⚠ **A shader of its own, and not a third `Pass` of the one below, for a reason a device found.**
/// A descriptor set is written whole or not at all, so a variant that shared a set with the passes
/// that sample a volume would have to bind *something* into that sampled slot — and the only 3D
/// textures in the frame at this point are the ones these passes write. Pointing a sampled binding at
/// a storage image the same dispatch writes asks the driver for one image in two layouts at once,
/// which the validation layers reject per dispatch and a release driver does not. The permutation
/// does not fold the binding away either: the layers report `source` and `volumeSampler` as
/// statically used in the variant that never calls them. Two shaders is what actually separates them.
shader VolumetricFogInject {
    [Permutation] val GridX: int = 160
    [Permutation] val GridY: int = 90
    [Permutation] val GridZ: int = 64

    /// Whether density thins with altitude, for a valley that fills from the bottom.
    [Permutation] val HeightFalloff: bool = true

    /// Scattering in `rgb`, extinction in `a`.
    [Format("rgba16f")] var target: RWTexture3D<float4>

    var inverseView: mat4
    var tanHalfFov: float2 = float2(1f, 1f)
    var fogNear: float = 0.5f
    var fogFar: float = 64f
    var density: float = 0.02f
    var scatteringAlbedo: float3 = float3(0.9f, 0.92f, 0.95f)
    var fogHeight: float = 0f
    var heightFalloffRate: float = 0.05f

    [ComputeShader(8, 8, 1)]
    func Main([Semantic("SV_DispatchThreadID")] id: uint3) {
        val x = int(id.x)
        val y = int(id.y)
        val z = int(id.z)

        if (x >= GridX || y >= GridY || z >= GridZ) {
            return
        }

        val positionVS = FroxelGrid.Centre(x, y, z, GridX, GridY, GridZ, fogNear, fogFar, tanHalfFov)
        val world = Math.TransformPoint(inverseView, positionVS)

        // `Fog.rvn`'s `HeightDensity`, and it has to be: the analytic fallback and the volume meet at
        // the far plane, and a valley that thins at one rate inside and another beyond it is a
        // visible ring at exactly the distance the two swap over.
        val altitude = HeightFalloff ? exp(-max(world.y - fogHeight, 0f) * heightFalloffRate) : 1f

        // Two numbers rather than one, because they are two physical quantities and only their ratio
        // is the albedo. Extinction is what leaves the straight-through ray — scattering *and*
        // absorption — and scattering is the part that comes back somewhere else.
        val extinction = max(density * altitude, 0f)

        target.Store(int3(x, y, z), float4(scatteringAlbedo * extinction, extinction))
    }
}

shader VolumetricFog {
    /// 0 lights the medium, 1 marches it. The injection is `VolumetricFogInject` above.
    [Permutation] val Pass: int = 0

    /// How many froxels across, down and deep. The host declares volumes of exactly this shape.
    [Permutation] val GridX: int = 160
    [Permutation] val GridY: int = 90
    [Permutation] val GridZ: int = 64

    /// Whether the sun's cascades are consulted per froxel — the difference between glow and shafts.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **The whole reason the volume exists.** Unshadowed, a froxel grid computes the same
    /// in-scatter an analytic falloff already gives for a fraction of the cost, and the only thing it
    /// buys is a height gradient `Fog.rvn` also has. A beam is the *absence* of light behind a
    /// caster, so it cannot be derived from distance and altitude at all — something has to ask, per
    /// point in the air, whether the sun reaches it.
    ///
    /// ⚠ Availability and not preference. The host turns this on when the frame actually published
    /// cascades and an atlas, on `TerrainSceneRenderer.DetectMode`'s rule — a frame with no shadow
    /// node has nothing to sample and no toggle can conjure it.
    [Permutation] val Shadowed: bool = false

    /// The volume this dispatch writes: the lit medium, then the marched result.
    [Format("rgba16f")] var target: RWTexture3D<float4>

    /// The volume it reads: the medium, then the scattering. Sampled by both of these passes.
    var source: Texture3D

    /// How the volumes are read. Point-equivalent at texel centres, which is where every read lands.
    var volumeSampler: Sampler

    /// The camera's half-angle tangents, which turn a grid UV into a view ray.
    ///
    /// ⚠ Not derivable here. The projection matrix is not bound and inverting one to recover two
    /// numbers the host already has is two derivations of one quantity — `ClusterGrid.UvOf` states
    /// the same rule about the same pair.
    var tanHalfFov: float2 = float2(1f, 1f)

    /// Where the grid's first slice begins, in metres of view depth.
    ///
    /// ⚠ Not zero and not the camera's near plane by accident: `SliceDepth` is a ratio raised to a
    /// power, so a near of zero makes every slice zero and a near of a millimetre spends a third of
    /// the grid inside the camera.
    var fogNear: float = 0.5f

    /// Where its last slice ends. The volume covers nothing beyond this; the composite falls back.
    var fogFar: float = 64f

    /// Which way the light travels, and what it carries.
    var sunDirection: float3 = float3(0f, -1f, 0f)
    var sunColour: float3 = float3(1f, 0.9f, 0.7f)

    /// Henyey–Greenstein anisotropy. Air's forward peak is what makes a sunbeam a sunbeam.
    var phaseG: float = 0.7f

    /// What arrives from the whole sky.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Without it a valley with the sun behind the viewer is a hole in the world**, which is the
    /// failure `WaterShading.AmbientInScatter` records at length and for the same reason: a phase
    /// function is normalised over the sphere, so one directional light contributes almost nothing
    /// outside its forward peak. Light arriving uniformly from everywhere is scattered uniformly
    /// whatever `phaseG` is, because the phase integrates to one — so this term has no phase factor.
    var ambientColour: float3 = float3(0.35f, 0.42f, 0.55f)

    // --- The sun's shadow, as the scene pass already published it ------------------

    /// View to world, for putting a froxel where the cascades were fitted.
    ///
    /// ⚠ The same matrix `VolumetricFogInject` was given, from the same host derivation. Two
    /// inversions of one camera is a lighting pass shadowing froxels the injection put elsewhere.
    var inverseView: mat4

    /// The sun's cascades, tile folded in — `ShadowMapRenderer.Publish`'s matrices verbatim.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Four, fixed, with the tail padded degenerate**, on `FrameLit.rvn`'s contract: a struct
    /// function's array parameter cannot be sized by a permutation, and a zero matrix fails
    /// `FrameShadow.Containing`'s `clip.w > 0` test, so a padded slot can never be selected.
    var cascades: ShadowCascade[4]

    /// The cascade atlas the frame's shadow node rendered.
    var shadowMap: Texture2D

    /// How it is read. Unfiltered and clamped — the compare in `Lighting.ShadowTap` is a compare,
    /// and a filtered depth is the average of two casters, which is a depth nothing is at.
    var shadowSampler: Sampler

    /// The *atlas's* texel, not a tile's. The taps step in coordinates the tile transform made
    /// atlas-wide, so a tile-sized texel would spread a 3×3 filter across four cascades.
    var shadowTexelSize: float2 = float2(1f, 1f)

    var shadowConstantBias: float = 0.008f
    var shadowSlopeBias: float = 0.01f
    var shadowFadeRange: float = 10f

    [ComputeShader(8, 8, 1)]
    func Main([Semantic("SV_DispatchThreadID")] id: uint3) {
        val x = int(id.x)
        val y = int(id.y)

        if (x >= GridX || y >= GridY) {
            return
        }

        if (Pass == 1) {
            Integrate(x, y)
            return
        }

        val z = int(id.z)

        if (z >= GridZ) {
            return
        }

        Scatter(x, y, z)
    }

    /// The grid UV a froxel's centre sits at, with this variant's dimensions filled in.
    func GridUv(x: int, y: int): float2 => FroxelGrid.Uv(x, y, GridX, GridY)

    /// The texel-centre UVW of a froxel, for a read of one of the volumes.
    func VolumeUvw(x: int, y: int, z: int): float3 {
        return float3(
            (float(x) + 0.5f) / float(GridX),
            (float(y) + 0.5f) / float(GridY),
            (float(z) + 0.5f) / float(GridZ)
        )
    }

    // --- Pass 0: what the medium does with the light --------------------------------

    /// Writes the radiance scattered toward the camera from one froxel, and carries the extinction.
    ///
    /// A radiance *density* and not a path integral: the marching is `Integrate`'s, and doing it here
    /// too would count the same slab twice.
    func Scatter(x: int, y: int, z: int) {
        val medium = source.SampleLevel(volumeSampler, VolumeUvw(x, y, z), 0f)
        val scattering = medium.rgb
        val extinction = medium.a

        // From the froxel toward the camera, which is the direction the scattered light leaves in.
        val toCamera = -Math.SafeNormalize(FroxelGrid.Ray(GridUv(x, y), tanHalfFov))
        val toSun = Math.SafeNormalize(-sunDirection)

        // The angle between where the light came *from* and where it is going. `WaterVolume.Phase`
        // takes it in exactly these terms.
        val cosTheta = dot(toCamera, toSun)

        // ⚠ **On the direct term only.** The ambient is what arrives from the whole sky, and a
        // froxel the sun cannot reach still sees most of it — multiplying both by one visibility
        // makes every shadowed pocket of air black, which is the hole `ambientColour` exists to stop.
        val direct = sunColour * WaterVolume.Phase(cosTheta, phaseG) * SunVisibility(x, y, z)
        val inscatter = scattering * (direct + ambientColour)

        target.Store(int3(x, y, z), float4(inscatter, extinction))
    }

    /// How much of the sun reaches a froxel: 1 in the open, 0 behind a caster.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **`NdotL` is 1, and that is the physics rather than a shortcut.** `FrameShadow.Term`'s two
    /// normal-derived corrections both exist to fight self-shadowing on a *surface*: the slope bias
    /// grows where a face is oblique to the light, and the normal offset walks the sample point off
    /// the face it sits on. A froxel is a volume of air with no face to be oblique to and no surface
    /// to lift off, so both must vanish — and at `NdotL == 1` they do, exactly and by their own
    /// arithmetic (`sqrt(1 − 1) == 0` and `saturate(1 − 1) == 0`). What is left is the constant bias
    /// scaled by the cascade's depth scale, which is the only one a medium wants. Passing a real
    /// normal here would bias the air by a slope it does not have.
    ///
    /// Reverse-Z throughout: `Lighting.ShadowTap` holds the `depth >= stored` compare and the bias is
    /// ADDED, because a larger depth is nearer the light.
    ///
    /// Everything else `Term` does is wanted unchanged — containment selection, the blend across the
    /// last tenth of a cascade, and the fade to unshadowed at the shadow distance. A shaft that ended
    /// in a hard line at a cascade seam is the artefact that rule was written for.
    func SunVisibility(x: int, y: int, z: int): float {
        if (!Shadowed) {
            return 1f
        }

        val positionVS = FroxelGrid.Centre(x, y, z, GridX, GridY, GridZ, fogNear, fogFar, tanHalfFov)
        val world = Math.TransformPoint(inverseView, positionVS)

        return FrameShadow.Term(
            shadowMap,
            shadowSampler,
            cascades,
            world,
            float3(0f, 1f, 0f),
            1f,
            FroxelGrid.DepthOf(positionVS),
            shadowTexelSize,
            shadowConstantBias,
            shadowSlopeBias,
            shadowFadeRange
        )
    }

    // --- Pass 1: the march ---------------------------------------------------

    /// Marches one column of froxels front to back, accumulating in-scatter and transmittance.
    ///
    /// Serial in `z` and one invocation per column, which is why this pass dispatches a flat grid: the
    /// accumulation is a prefix sum along the ray, and every froxel's answer depends on every froxel
    /// in front of it. A parallel scan would be correct and would cost more than the sixty-four steps
    /// it replaced.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **The in-scatter is integrated over the slab rather than multiplied by its length.** For a
    /// slab of constant extinction the integral of `S · exp(−σt · s)` from zero to `d` is
    /// `S · (1 − exp(−σt · d)) / σt`, and the difference from `S · d` is the whole reason a dense fog
    /// does not glow: past a few extinction lengths a thicker slab scatters no more light toward the
    /// camera, because what it scatters in at the far end it absorbs again on the way out.
    /// `WaterVolume.InScatter`'s remarks are the same observation for a lake.
    func Integrate(x: int, y: int) {
        // How much further a metre along the ray is than a metre of view depth. `Ray`'s z is −1, so
        // its length is exactly that ratio — and without it every froxel off the screen's centre
        // integrates a slab shorter than the one the light crossed, which reads as fog that thins
        // toward the corners.
        val rayScale = length(FroxelGrid.Ray(GridUv(x, y), tanHalfFov))

        var accumulated = float3(0f, 0f, 0f)
        var transmittance = 1f

        for (z in 0 .. GridZ - 1) {
            val slab = source.SampleLevel(volumeSampler, VolumeUvw(x, y, z), 0f)
            val extinction = max(slab.a, 0f)

            val near = FroxelGrid.SliceDepth(z, GridZ, fogNear, fogFar)
            val far = FroxelGrid.SliceDepth(z + 1, GridZ, fogNear, fogFar)
            val step = max(far - near, 0f) * rayScale

            val stepTransmittance = exp(-extinction * step)

            // The closed form above, with the `σt → 0` limit written out: an empty froxel's integral
            // is `S · d`, and the quotient would be zero over zero.
            val integral = extinction > Const.Epsilon ? (1f - stepTransmittance) / extinction : step

            accumulated = accumulated + transmittance * slab.rgb * integral
            transmittance = transmittance * stepTransmittance

            target.Store(int3(x, y, z), float4(accumulated, transmittance))
        }
    }
}
